   127	## 3. The second-generation tests: statement, licence, result
   128	
   129	All tests act on the child tower `(n', m', M'_1..M'_{s'}, d', V'_2..V'_{s'}, delta', l)` of the
   130	roster, with `V'_{s'+1} := 1` on complete chains (`d'_{s'+1} = 1`: "all `n'` roots lie in `D'_{s'}`"
   131	is a tautology, not the p.201(4) convention). On prefixes the top level is untested.
   132	
   133	**(W) Child window.** `P'_j = V'_{j+1} d'_j/d'_{j+1} ≥ V'_j > d'_j/(n'−M'_j)` — Def 5.1(2) p.179,
   134	p.201 (7). Already 65/65 in the own-data gate; re-confirmed 66/66.
   135	
   136	**(GO) Child Galois pattern and ODE at level `j`.** With `L'_j = lcm{den delta'_i : i > j}` and
   137	`A'_j = den(L'_j delta'_j)` (p.201 (8), verbatim "let the l.c.m. of the reduced denominators of
   138	`delta_s, …, delta_r` be `L`; `A_{r−1}` is the reduced denominator of `L delta_{r−1}`"), a pattern
   139	`p'(xi) = xi^z ∏(xi^{A'} − c_nu)^{r_nu}`, `z + A'Σr = P'_j`, must exist with `V'_j ∈ {z} ∪ {r_nu}`
   140	(p.201 (10),(11)), `[z>0] + A'·#orbits ≤ Q'_j` (Prop 4.6(3),(4): `q` squarefree, roots of `p` are
   141	roots of `q`), no multiplicity equal to `P'_j/Q'_j` (from A.3's identity `P p q' − Q p' q = c p`
   142	at a root of multiplicity `v`: `(P − Qv) q'(a) = c ≠ 0`; this also excludes `p = q^w`, 4.6(5)),
   143	and some multiplicity `> P'_j/Q'_j` (A.3(4)). **Licence for the child:** the p.171 Remark,
   144	verbatim "*With a verbatim proof, for a slightly general Jacobian condition … `J_{x,y}(f,g) = x^l`
   145	Proposition 4.6 is still valid with the condition (3) replaced by (3)*: `λ = (−1−l+δ)/(n−m_r)`*",
   146	and the child is exactly such a pair by Prop 6.3(3) (`J_{γ,π} = −(u_s/b) γ^{v_s−u_s−1}`). The
   147	Galois orbit statement is field theory over `k((t'))` and needs no Jacobian.
   148	Result: 66/66 alive at every level (pattern counts 1–36 per level, `second_gen.out`).
   149	
   150	**(B) Child-bottom divisibility.** p.188, verbatim: "*if the reduced denominator `A` of `δ_1` is
   151	not a factor of `deg g_σ(π) = n*V_2` then `π` is a factor of `g_σ(π)`. Moreover if `A` is a factor
   152	of `n*V_2` then `g_σ(π) ∈ k[π^A]` and `π` is a factor of `(d/dπ) g_σ(π)`. In view of the two co-prime
   153	conditions above we must have `A | n*V_2 and A ∤ m*V_2 or A ∤ n*V_2 and A | m*V_2`. Moreover we
   154	always have from the very definition of `A` the following `A | (n*+m*)V_2 − 1`*" — the last line
   155	is the `π → ζπ` covariance of the r = 1 ODE `D(n, −M_1, g_σ, T_{1,σ}) = c ≠ 0` (Prop 4.6, last
